int and the second point is like  i mentioned  we don't have to have the governing  equations and galerkin project we can  also build these f these dynamical  systems models for z dot equals f of z  using kind of machine learning  regressions things like dynamic mode  decomposition or cindy the sparse  identification of nonlinear dynamics or  an rnn an lstm or an echo state network  so this is really nice you can kind of  pick modularly i can  use linear coordinates and use some of  these modern machine learning models  or i can use non-linear coordinates and  i can either do classical galerkin  projection or again use some of these  modern modeling techniques  and so there's lots and lots of  different ways you can combine these  things  one of the really powerful recent  methods is by lee and karl berg this is  a 2020 jcp paper  where they basically show that you can  get much better reduced order model  performance using these deep  uh  these deep convolutional auto encoders  to extract kind of multi-scale features  in a non-linear way as opposed to the  the proper orthogonal decomposition i  showed you earlier  uh and they you know they do this for  lots and lots of different test systems  this one i think is a system that has  some uh  you know  some  stiff physics in this in the system  and the high fidelity simulation in  yellow that's our ground truth  all of the classic pod roms are these  jiggly solutions they actually do a  really poor job  away from this transition  whereas using these deep coordinate  systems here actually allow you you can  barely even see it because it perfectly  lays on top  of the high fidelity simulation  so either deep galerkin rom that's  galerkin projection or this lspg rom  that's their kind of custom  uh galerkin ron this this least squares  petrov galerkin  rom  do much better when you use this deep  convolutional uh coordinate system  so using classical galerkin models is  still improved dramatically by using  these deep convolutional autoencoders  another  kind of the flip side of that is okay  what if i don't want to use the  governing equations to go lurk and  project the dynamics what if that's  really expensive and i don't want to  take a working code to do this galerkin  projection  jared callahan and john kristoff lazzo  showed that similarly for these complex  fluid examples you can use this  autoencoder structure to learn a very  very low dimensional latent space for  example in the shear driven cavity  and you can use regression like sparse  identification of nonlinear dynamics  this cindy approach  to learn regression models for the  dynamics in the latent space  so you don't need any access to the  governing equations you don't need to  know that there's a navy or stokes  equation you don't need a working  simulation  to do any of this you just need data  to build an auto encoder to find that  latent space and then you can build  these regression models in that latent  space  this is another really really cool vein  of work  by by baron nowak mark  luke pasteur  and they're a fantastic phd student nan  deng  i was fortunate enough to be on on his  committee and learn about all of his  great work  where essentially they have done a  similar approach  where they are learning these cindy  models these sparse non-linear models  for this very complex flow past the  fluidic pinball this three cylinder  configuration  and this example is so cool as you  increase the reynolds number the  reynolds number of the system  it goes through a number of pretty  sophisticated bifurcations um so it goes  through the hops bifurcation a pitchfork  knee mark sacker all the way to chaos  and they're able to capture this rich  rich phenomenology as you increase the  flow velocity you get all of these  different phenomena they're able to  capture it by using these modern sparse  model identification techniques to build  these reduced order models  so very powerful stuff  now going back to the um  oh yeah this one is a bit of a different  flavor and this is just working i have  to show you because it's one of my  favorite papers out there this is from  2014 so kind of the early days of  machine learning this is not deep  learning this is work by erica kaiser  bear noack and friends  and the idea is  that you can use relatively simple  techniques in clustering to build that  low dimensional latent space that low  dimensional variable that i was talking  about  so here in the lorentz system if you do  something like a k-means clustering you  can break this up into 10 different  clusters  and the center of those clusters whether  or not you're in one of these different  rainbow  regions  is the state of your system  and so you can basically take a long  time trajectory and you can figure out  how often you transition from state 1 to  state 2 or state 2 to state 3 and so on  and so forth  and you can build a probability model a  transition state model for how the  system probabilistically transitions  from these clusters  and i think this is just a brilliant way  of reduced order modeling very complex  chaotic systems and reducing it to  relatively simple linear transition  dynamics  and erica  showed that you can do this on much more  complex systems very high dimensional  nonlinear fluid systems like this mixing  layer that has you know maybe a hundred  thousand or a million degrees of freedom  you can still cluster your phase space  you can still do data driven clustering  to find you know 10 or 20 clusters  and count the probability of  transitioning between those clusters  and when you do graph analysis on this  you learn really interesting things  about your physics for example that  there is a kelvin-helmholtz  mechanism that's the transition from two  to three to four  and there's vortex pairing that's  another set of transitions and there's  this what they call the flipper mode  between these two different  phenomenologies  so you know really powerful things you  can do with relatively simple machine  learning i mean this is you know k means  clustering one of the textbook examples  you can build these beautiful reduced  order models for complex systems  okay the last thing i'll show you and  this is actually halfway between  turbulence modeling and reduced order  modeling  this is some fantastic work by sarah  beatham and jessie capisilatro  where they again use that cindy that  sparse nonlinear model identification  technique but instead of building roms  they use it to build  they use it to build turbulence closure  models  so they essentially are modeling the  terms in the turbulence closure and the  rand's equations using these sparse  polynomial or tensorial expressions  and so what they get out and they don't  just use cindy they also look at gene  expression programming as an alternative  method to get these interpretable  expressions  what they get out are turbulence closure  models that can be read by a human their  their analytic expressions equations you  can write down and you can analyze and  study and do analysis on  which is really really cool  um and i've i just realized  learned recently um that there is also  work doing this in in climate science so  maybe i'll talk about that at some point  okay  so that was a a whirlwind tour through  this review paper and believe it or not  that was only a small fraction of the  the things we we talked about so ricardo  vinuesa and myself in this archive paper  on how you can use machine learning  to accelerate direct numerical  simulations to improve high fidelity  simulations  to improve kind of medium fidelity  turbulence closure modeling  and how to uh improve  kind of the scaling and the  interpretability and just the general  operation of these you know  very very tailored reduced order models  so again from highest fidelity to lowest  fidelity there are many many  opportunities to embed machine learning  into the process and to get better  computational fluid models  so a few takeaways that i think are  really important and we talk about these  a little bit too  we as a community  have to be committed to reproducible  research to open source codes to open  source data and to good benchmark  problems we need to have agreed upon  benchmark problems in these areas so  that when we develop new machine  learning techniques we can compare them  in a head-to-head kind of  apples-to-apples way on these different  canonical problems  and that means we have to be open and  have best practices for reproducible and  open research  another thing that i think is incredibly  important is when you are saying if you  if you want to claim that you are using  machine learning to improve direct  numerical simulations maybe you get  faster models  you have to include the  time it took to generate your training  data  okay because that oftentimes is  neglected when we talk about the  numerical scaling of these machine  learning solutions  and it's often  very very expensive to build the  training data that you need to train  your your models  now in some cases like in reduced order  models i don't mind if i have a very  expensive offline training phase as long  as my model is very fast online when i  use it you know for my flying quadrotor  or whatever i'm using it for  but if you're claiming to use machine  learning to actually improve simulations  direct numerical simulations  you know sometimes it might still be  faster to just compute the direct  numerical simulation if you need enough  training data and it takes so much time  to generate those models  unless your models are general enough  that you can use them lots of times in  the future okay so that's another really  important point is you have to be very  careful when you're doing these  comparisons  and maybe the last point i'll make is  these methods have been optimized by  really smart people for you know decades  and decades and decades right people  have been developing  just incredible algorithms for  all three of these uh these pieces of  computational fluid dynamics for you  know 50 years or more  and so it's very hard for machine  learning to come in and out of the gate  and beat these approaches  and so i think we have to be willing to  have kind of a learning curve in how we  use these machine learning algorithms in  these fields you can't expect these to  immediately built beat the state of the  art  so if you get promising results we have  to be critical on the one hand um you  know because these there are such good  existing methods  but we also have to think you know how  are these machine learning methods going  to look five years from now 10 years  from now if they keep getting the same  development that these classical  approaches have had  okay so if you're interested in this  topic i really encourage you to check  out this paper there's a ton more  examples there  and this is such a fast growing field  you know in a year we're going to need  to have a whole new set of examples so  really great area to be doing research  in  and i hope you all enjoy this and you  know have some ideas of your own to to  get started with all right thank you
 

